## 2. PHASE 0 — AUDIT THE ACTUAL REPOSITORY FIRST

Before writing or editing anything:

1. Verify repository identity, owner/name, visibility, default branch, current HEAD, latest commit, working tree if a local checkout is available, permissions, branch/PR policy, and tool capability.
2. Enumerate all files and folders. Check docs, contracts, schemas, test fixtures, source files, package manifests, lockfiles, CI/workflows, issue templates, README, ADRs, GitHub issues and PRs.
3. Read the entire current version of each relevant document, not only snippets:
   - `README.md`;
   - master Design System Bible;
   - Theme Contract;
   - Skin Grammar Contract and any 105-pair review matrix;
   - Token Dictionary Contract;
   - Component API & Behavior Bible/manifest, if present;
   - Premium Blocks & Template Product Bible/catalog, if present;
   - repository audit/research log, if present;
   - implementation roadmap;
   - any existing table/data-workspace specifications;
   - GitHub issue backlog and pull requests, if accessible.
4. Verify all document links, filenames, IDs, status vocabularies, schema versions and references. Find obsolete links and duplicate/conflicting definitions.
5. Determine actual status for every relevant item:
   - `verified-existing`;
   - `specified-not-implemented`;
   - `partially-specified`;
   - `missing`;
   - `contradictory`;
   - `stale-needs-research`;
   - `implemented-and-tested` (only with code and test evidence).
6. If source code exists, audit current React/TypeScript/CSS/Tailwind/build/test/library versions and current patterns. If no product code exists, say so explicitly; do not invent a package setup.
7. Review previous decisions and open issues before proposing new architecture. Avoid creating another file that says the same thing as an existing canonical doc.
8. Produce an evidence-based report, for example `docs/audit/advanced-data-workspace-audit.md`, containing:
   - commit/branch and audit date;
   - files reviewed;
   - current coverage and status;
   - missing/overlapping/contradictory scope;
   - risks and severity P0/P1/P2/P3;
   - evidence paths/sections/URLs;
   - recommended changes;
   - explicitly out-of-scope decisions.
9. Continue to research and improve the repo after writing the audit, unless access is blocked or a genuinely destructive decision needs owner approval.

### Repository hygiene

- Do not silently remove or overwrite an existing file because a new name seems cleaner.
- Prefer targeted updates over duplicate versions. If a new specialized Bible is necessary, define how it relates to the master Bible and component catalog.
- If creating schemas, also provide small valid and invalid fixtures and prove validation runs.
- Use existing package manager/test tooling if present. Do not invent commands before auditing the repository.

